{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And Yitzhak was forty years old.</b> Rashi: he waited for her until she was fit for intercourse — three years old — and then married her. It appears otherwise, for at the end of Sifrei the conclusion is three — their years are equal: Rivka, Kehat, and Ben Azzai, and they lived one hundred and thirty-three years. And if you suppose that Rivka was only three years old when she married Yitzhak, it turns out she is short eleven years from her total. How so? Three years old when she married, and twenty-three when she gave birth, as Rashi explained, and Yaakov was sixty-three years old when he was blessed, as Rashi explained, and fourteen years he was hidden in the house of Shem and Ever, and twenty years he served in the house of Lavan, and a year and a half in Sukkot, and six months in Bet El — in that passage he was informed of his mother's death, as Rashi explained at אלון בכות. Add twenty-three, how old she was when she gave birth, to ninety-nine years from when she gave birth until she died — that yields only one hundred twenty-two. Therefore it appears she was fourteen years old when she married. And so we learn in Seder Olam: when Avraham returned from the Akeidah, he was informed that Rivka had been born — meaning he was informed she had already been born when eleven years had passed. And a proof for the matter: ויקרא לנערה וישאלה את פיה — Rashi explained that one does not give a grown woman in marriage except with her consent." "<b>Of Padan Aram.</b> Rashi: the field of Aram; and in the language of Yishmael, פדן means field. About him his friend declares: ויברח יעקב שדה ארם." "<b>Sister of Lavan the Aramean.</b> It is the way of Scripture to relate a woman after her older brother, as in אחות נביות, אחות אהרן, אחות נחשון."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:05:54.815546148Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}